// ===== CURRICULUM PART 7: Classic regions in depth =====
L.push({id:"r1b", unit:"R1", title:"Bordeaux in Depth: Growths, Châteaux and the Place", mins:22,
summary:"The 1855 list and how the market wrote it, the first growths one by one, second wines, and how la Place and en primeur work.",
body:`
<p>At Certified and beyond, "Bordeaux" stops being a region and becomes a list of names. Candidates are expected to place châteaux in their growth and commune, and to understand how Bordeaux is sold, because the business model shapes the wine world.</p>

<p>The names are easier to hold when each tier is understood as a piece of commercial history. The classification, the second wines and the futures market all come from one source: for three hundred years the leading estates of Bordeaux have been brands, sold by reputation to buyers who would never see the vineyard. This chapter gives the lists and then the story behind each.</p>

<h3>How the first growths became first</h3>
<p>The top tier of 1855 was not decided in 1855. It had been fixed by the market for a century and a half. The earliest evidence is from London. In April 1663 the diarist Samuel Pepys recorded drinking at a tavern "a sort of French wine called Ho Bryan, that hath a good and most particular taste that I never met with." It is the first known mention in English of a Bordeaux wine by the name of its estate. The owner of Haut-Brion, Arnaud de Pontac, president of the Bordeaux parliament, understood what he had: in 1666 he sent his son to London to open a tavern, the Pontack's Head, where the wine sold for several times the price of ordinary claret.</p>
<p>Others followed. By the first decade of the eighteenth century, Margaux, Lafite and Latour were being sold at London auctions under their own names, at prices three and four times those of other wine. For a period one man, the Marquis Nicolas-Alexandre de Ségur, owned Lafite, Latour, Mouton and Calon at once; Louis XV is said to have called him the Prince of Vines. When Thomas Jefferson, then the American minister to France, toured Bordeaux in May 1787, he wrote down what the merchants told him: there were four vineyards of first quality, Margaux, Latour, Lafite and Haut-Brion. He listed second and third ranks below them. The structure of 1855 already existed.</p>
<p>So when the Bordeaux brokers were asked in April 1855 to supply a list of the region's best wines for the Paris exhibition, they did not taste anything. They consulted their price records and wrote down the hierarchy the trade had long used. That is why Haut-Brion, alone among Graves estates, appears on a list otherwise drawn from the Médoc: its price put it there.</p>

<h3>The 1855 Médoc (and Haut-Brion) red classification</h3>
<table class="t"><tr><th>Growth</th><th>Châteaux</th></tr>
<tr><td>First (5)</td><td>Lafite Rothschild, Latour, Margaux, Haut-Brion, Mouton Rothschild (promoted 1973)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Second (14)</td><td>Rauzan-Ségla, Rauzan-Gassies, Léoville-Las Cases, Léoville-Poyferré, Léoville-Barton, Durfort-Vivens, Gruaud-Larose, Lascombes, Brane-Cantenac, Pichon-Longueville Baron, Pichon Longueville Comtesse de Lalande, Ducru-Beaucaillou, Cos d'Estournel, Montrose</td></tr>
<tr><td>Third (14)</td><td>Kirwan, d'Issan, Lagrange, Langoa-Barton, Giscours, Malescot St-Exupéry, Boyd-Cantenac, Cantenac-Brown, Palmer, La Lagune, Desmirail, Calon-Ségur, Ferrière, Marquis d'Alesme</td></tr>
<tr><td>Fourth (10)</td><td>Saint-Pierre, Talbot, Branaire-Ducru, Duhart-Milon, Pouget, La Tour Carnet, Lafon-Rochet, Beychevelle, Prieuré-Lichine, Marquis de Terme</td></tr>
<tr><td>Fifth (18)</td><td>Pontet-Canet, Batailley, Haut-Batailley, Grand-Puy-Lacoste, Grand-Puy-Ducasse, Lynch-Bages, Lynch-Moussas, Dauzac, d'Armailhac, du Tertre, Haut-Bages Libéral, Pédesclaux, Belgrave, Camensac, Cos Labory, Clerc Milon, Croizet-Bages, Cantemerle</td></tr></table>
<p>The growths reflect 1855 prices, not today's quality. "Super seconds" such as Léoville-Las Cases, Pichon Comtesse, Ducru-Beaucaillou, Cos d'Estournel, Montrose and Palmer (a third) often sell near first-growth level. Fifths like Lynch-Bages and Pontet-Canet (biodynamic) outperform their rank.</p>

<p>A few features of the table are worth fixing. The communes are unevenly represented: Margaux has the most classed growths, twenty-one, and Pauillac the most first growths, three, while Saint-Julien has no first but five seconds, which is why its average is so high. Saint-Estèphe has only five classed growths in all. Several names recur because single estates were divided by inheritance: the three Léovilles were one property, as were the two Pichons and the two Rauzans.</p>
<p><b>Mouton Rothschild</b> is the single promotion. Ranked first among the seconds in 1855, though its price was close to the firsts, it was bought by the Rothschild family in 1853 and taken over in 1922 by Baron Philippe de Rothschild, then twenty. He insisted from 1924 that the whole crop be bottled at the château, commissioned an artist's label each year from 1945, and campaigned for half a century to have the ranking changed. In 1973 the agriculture minister, Jacques Chirac, signed the decree. The estate's motto had been "First I cannot be, second I do not deign to be, I am Mouton." It became "First I am, second I was, Mouton does not change."</p>
<p>The term <b>super second</b> is the trade's own correction to the list. It names estates, mostly seconds, whose wines sell well above their rank and sometimes approach the firsts in quality. Its existence makes the central point about 1855: the classification is a record of prices, and prices have gone on moving.</p>

<h3>The five first growths, one by one</h3>
<p>The firsts share a rank and differ in character, and the differences follow from site and blend.</p>
<p><b>Lafite Rothschild</b>, at the northern edge of Pauillac on the border with Saint-Estèphe, is the largest of the five and the most restrained. Its vineyard lies on deep, fine gravel over limestone, and the wine, very high in Cabernet Sauvignon, is known for perfume and length more than weight: cedar, graphite, a lightness of touch that can seem understated when young. The Rothschild family has owned it since 1868.</p>
<p><b>Latour</b> stands at the southern end of Pauillac, a few hundred meters from the estuary. Its core is a single walled parcel, l'Enclos, on a gravel mound with clay beneath, close enough to the water to escape most frosts. It gives the most powerful, tannic and slowest-maturing of the firsts, and has a reputation for succeeding in poor years, when the clay keeps the vines supplied and the site ripens Cabernet that fails elsewhere.</p>
<p><b>Mouton Rothschild</b>, between the two, is the most flamboyant: riper, more opulent, with exotic spice and a rich texture alongside the Pauillac cassis.</p>
<p><b>Château Margaux</b>, the only first in its commune, is the most fragrant. The thin, pale gravel of Margaux gives wine of lower weight and finer tannin, with a floral, violet character over the dark fruit. It also makes a white, Pavillon Blanc, from Sauvignon Blanc, sold as plain Bordeaux because the Margaux appellation is for red wine only.</p>
<p><b>Haut-Brion</b>, in Pessac, is the outsider: the oldest name, the only one outside the Médoc, the smallest, and the one with the most Merlot in the blend. It ripens earliest, being inside the warmth of the city, and its wine is the most approachable young, with a smoky, earthy, tobacco note particular to the Graves. Its neighbor and stablemate La Mission Haut-Brion is regarded as a first growth in all but name.</p>

<h3>Who owns the châteaux</h3>
<p>Ownership has shifted in the last fifty years from old Bordeaux families to outside capital, and it matters because money decides how severe an estate can afford to be. Rothschild branches own Lafite and Mouton. Latour belongs to the holding company of François Pinault, which also controls luxury brands. Margaux has belonged to the Mentzelopoulos family since 1977, and Haut-Brion to the American Dillon family since 1935. Yquem and Cheval Blanc are part of the luxury group LVMH. Many second to fifth growths belong to insurance companies, banks and industrial families from France and abroad. An owner who does not depend on each year's sales can discard half the crop in a poor vintage, replant at leisure and hold stock back. The widening gap between the top estates and the rest of Bordeaux is partly a gap in capital.</p>

<h3>Sauternes and Barsac (1855)</h3>
<p><b>Premier Cru Supérieur:</b> d'Yquem. <b>First Growths (11):</b> La Tour Blanche, Lafaurie-Peyraguey, Clos Haut-Peyraguey, Rayne-Vigneau, Suduiraut, Coutet, Climens, Guiraud, Rieussec, Rabaud-Promis, Sigalas-Rabaud. Coutet and Climens are in Barsac, which may label as Barsac or Sauternes. Below them sit the Second Growths (e.g., Doisy-Daëne, Nairac, de Malle).</p>

<p>The brokers classified the sweet wines of Sauternes and Barsac at the same time and by the same method, in two ranks with one estate set above both. In 1855 these were among the most expensive wines in the world, more costly than most of the Médoc, and <b>Château d'Yquem</b> more costly than any. Yquem's position rests on site and on severity. It occupies the highest hill in Sauternes, picks berry by berry in as many as ten or more passes, and discards whatever does not meet its standard; in poor years, of which there have been ten since 1900, it has released no Yquem at all. Barsac lies on flatter land with limestone and red sand, and its wines, Climens and Coutet above all, are a little lighter and fresher than those of Sauternes proper.</p>

<h3>Second wines and how to read them</h3>
<p>Most classed growths make a second wine from younger vines or lots not chosen for the grand vin: Carruades de Lafite, Les Forts de Latour, Pavillon Rouge du Château Margaux, Le Petit Mouton, Le Clarence de Haut-Brion, Clos du Marquis (Las Cases), Réserve de la Comtesse, Alter Ego (Palmer). They are a sommelier's best tool for offering a famous name at a reachable price.</p>

<p>Second wines are as old as the first growths; the practice of setting aside the best vats for the grand vin is recorded in the eighteenth century. In their modern form they date from the 1960s to 1980s, as estates began to select more strictly. Les Forts de Latour was first sold with the 1966 vintage. The logic is economic as well as qualitative. Declassifying a weaker lot protects the reputation and price of the first wine, and the second wine recovers some of the value. Many estates now go further and make a third wine as well.</p>
<p>What a second wine is varies. In most cases it is made from the estate's younger vines and from lots rejected at blending, so it is lighter and matures sooner. A few are different: Clos du Marquis comes from a separate vineyard and is closer to a distinct wine, and Les Forts de Latour is largely from its own parcels. In good vintages the gap narrows, since even the rejected lots are ripe. In poor vintages it widens, because selection for the grand vin is at its most severe. For the same reason the second wine of a great estate in a great year is one of the more reliable purchases in Bordeaux.</p>

<h3>The Right Bank names</h3>
<ul><li><b>Pomerol:</b> Pétrus (nearly all Merlot on a blue-clay buttonhole), Le Pin, Lafleur, Vieux Château Certan, Trotanoy, L'Évangile, La Conseillante, L'Église-Clinet.</li>
<li><b>Saint-Émilion:</b> Premier Grand Cru Classé A in 2022: Figeac and Pavie. Other stars: Canon, Belair-Monange, Troplong Mondot, Valandraud, Tertre Rôtebœuf; and Ausone, Cheval Blanc, Angélus outside the classification since 2022.</li></ul>

<p>The Right Bank's leading names came to prominence by a different route. Pomerol was a district of small farms with no aristocratic estates and no place in the trade's rankings; its wines were sold mainly in Belgium. Its rise after 1945 was largely the work of one merchant family in Libourne, the Moueix, who bought and promoted Pétrus and several of its neighbors, and later of American critics who favored its rich, early-maturing style. Scale is the difference that matters to a buyer. A Médoc first growth makes fifteen to twenty thousand cases of grand vin a year. Pétrus makes about two and a half thousand, and Le Pin, on two hectares, a few hundred. Scarcity explains much of the price.</p>
<p>The <b>garage wines</b> of Saint-Émilion took the idea further in the 1990s. Le Pin, first made in 1979, was the model: a tiny plot, very low yields, very ripe fruit, new oak. Château Valandraud, begun in 1991 by Jean-Luc Thunevin with less than a hectare and literally made in a garage, became the best known, selling for more than classed growths within a few years. The movement was controversial and brief, but it changed how the Right Bank worked, pushing even established estates toward lower yields and riper fruit. Valandraud is now a Premier Grand Cru Classé.</p>

<h3>Pessac-Léognan</h3>
<p>Haut-Brion, La Mission Haut-Brion, Pape Clément, Smith Haut Lafitte, Domaine de Chevalier, Haut-Bailly. Famous also for barrel-fermented Sauvignon-Sémillon whites (Haut-Brion Blanc, Domaine de Chevalier Blanc).</p>

<p>Pessac-Léognan is the oldest part of the Bordeaux vineyard and now lies partly inside the city's suburbs; Haut-Brion and La Mission Haut-Brion face each other across a road surrounded by houses. The gravel here is among the deepest in the region, and the wines combine Médoc structure with a smoky, earthy, mineral character of their own that tasters describe as warm brick or tobacco. It is also the one district of Bordeaux where the dry whites are as serious as the reds.</p>

<h3>How Bordeaux is sold: la Place and en primeur</h3>
<p>Classed growths rarely sell directly. They release wine through <b>la Place de Bordeaux</b>: the château sells allocations to <b>négociants</b> through <b>courtiers</b> (brokers), and négociants sell to importers worldwide. Each spring after the harvest, critics taste barrel samples and châteaux release prices <b>en primeur</b> (as futures). Buyers pay now and receive bottles about two years later. When prices rise faster than the market, en primeur stalls, as happened in several recent campaigns. Some top non-Bordeaux wines (Opus One, Masseto, Almaviva, Seña) now also sell through la Place.</p>
<p><b>La Place</b> is not a place. It is the network of some hundred brokers and more than three hundred merchant houses through which the châteaux sell. Its origin is practical. For centuries the growers made wine and the merchants of the Chartrons quay aged, bottled and shipped it, since only they had the capital and the foreign contacts. The broker stood between the two, knew every cellar, and guaranteed that the wine delivered was the wine tasted, for a commission that is still fixed at two percent. When châteaux began bottling their own wine in the twentieth century the merchants stopped being makers and became distributors, but the three-part structure remained.</p>
<p>The system has real advantages. A château offers its wine to dozens of merchants at once at a single price, and within hours it is on sale in more than a hundred countries. The estate needs no sales staff and carries no risk of unpaid accounts. The cost is distance from the customer: the château does not know who drinks its wine and has little control over the price at which it finally sells.</p>
<p><b>En primeur</b> is the sale of that wine before it is bottled. Selling young wine in barrel is old, but the modern campaign, aimed at private buyers and driven by published scores, dates from the 1982 vintage. That year was large, ripe and excellent, the dollar was strong, and a young American critic, Robert Parker, championed it while established voices hesitated. Buyers who followed him made large profits as prices rose. For the next three decades the pattern held often enough to sustain the system: buy in the spring after the harvest, pay at once, receive the wine two years later at a price below its eventual market value.</p>
<p>The mechanics are these. In April the trade tastes barrel samples in Bordeaux. From late April to June the châteaux release their prices one by one, often in portions called <b>tranches</b>, raising the price of the second tranche if the first sells quickly. Merchants are given allocations based on what they bought in previous years, which obliges them to buy in weak vintages to keep their share of strong ones.</p>
<p>Everything depends on one condition: the release price must be lower than what the wine will be worth later. After the celebrated 2009 and 2010 vintages, released at record prices, that condition often failed. Buyers found that they could purchase mature, ready-to-drink vintages for less than the new wine, and stopped buying futures. Château Latour left the system altogether, announcing in 2012 that the 2011 would be its last vintage sold en primeur and that it would release its wines only when it judged them ready to drink. The campaigns for the 2023 and 2024 vintages brought price cuts of a third or more and still struggled, and several merchant houses are in financial difficulty.</p>
<p>The merchants have meanwhile found a new use for their network. In 1998 the Chilean wine Almaviva, a Rothschild joint venture, was offered through la Place; Opus One followed in 2004. Dozens of wines from Italy, California, Chile, Australia and elsewhere are now released through Bordeaux each September in a second campaign, bottled and ready to ship. The distribution system built for claret has become a marketplace for the world's fine wine.</p>
`,
floor:`<p>On a list-correction exam, the commune matters: Château Margaux is in Margaux, Latour/Lafite/Mouton in Pauillac, the Léovilles and Ducru in Saint-Julien, Cos and Montrose in Saint-Estèphe. Selling tip: offer a second wine of a first growth beside a fifth growth's grand vin and let the guest choose name or depth.</p>`,
analyst:`<p>Diploma D2 and MW business papers ask how en primeur and la Place work, who carries risk, and why the model is under pressure. Know the chain: château → courtier → négociant → importer → retailer.</p>`,
terms:[["Super second","Second growth (or similar) selling near first-growth prices."],["La Place de Bordeaux","Négociant-and-broker system distributing Bordeaux worldwide."],["En primeur","Selling wine as futures before bottling."],["Courtier","Broker between châteaux and négociants."],["Grand vin","A château's top wine."]],
quiz:[
[1,"How many châteaux are in the First Growth tier of 1855 reds?","Five",["Three","Seven","Ten"],"Lafite, Latour, Margaux, Haut-Brion, Mouton."],
[2,"Which château is a Third Growth that often prices like a super second?","Palmer",["Lynch-Bages","Pontet-Canet","Talbot"],"Margaux appellation."],
[2,"Which is a Fifth Growth?","Lynch-Bages",["Ducru-Beaucaillou","Cos d'Estournel","Léoville-Las Cases"],"The others are Seconds."],
[2,"Carruades is the second wine of:","Lafite Rothschild",["Château Latour","Château Margaux","Mouton Rothschild"],"Les Forts de Latour is Latour's."],
[1,"En primeur means:","Selling wine as futures",["Bottling the wine at the estate","Using only the first pressing","Picking earlier than neighbors"],"The wine is sold in the spring after harvest, about 18 months before it is bottled and delivered."],
[3,"Which two Sauternes First Growths are in Barsac?","Climens and Coutet",["Suduiraut and Rieussec","Guiraud and La Tour Blanche","Rayne-Vigneau and Sigalas-Rabaud"],"Barsac may label as Sauternes."],
[2,"Pétrus is famous for nearly pure:","Merlot",["Cabernet Sauvignon","Cabernet Franc","Malbec"],"Blue clay in Pomerol."]
]});

L.push({id:"r2b", unit:"R1", title:"Burgundy Village by Village", mins:22,
summary:"Every Grand Cru and village of the Côte d'Or in order, with the reason each is where it is.",
body:`
<p>Burgundy is learned by walking it, north to south, village by village. Draw this route until you can do it from memory.</p>

<p>The route has a logic that makes it easier to hold. The Grands Crus are not scattered. They form two clusters, one of reds running almost without a break from Gevrey to Vosne, and one of whites around Montrachet, with the hill of Corton between them. Each village's wine has a character that follows from its soil and position, and the exceptions, the villages with no Grand Cru at all, each have a reason.</p>
<h3>Côte de Nuits: 24 Grand Crus, almost all red</h3>
<table class="t"><tr><th>Village</th><th>Grand Crus</th><th>Premiers Crus to know</th><th>Style</th></tr>
<tr><td>Marsannay, Fixin</td><td>None</td><td>Fixin Clos de la Perrière</td><td>Rustic, value; Marsannay rosé</td></tr>
<tr><td>Gevrey-Chambertin</td><td>Chambertin, Chambertin-Clos de Bèze, Chapelle-, Charmes-, Griotte-, Latricières-, Mazis-, Mazoyères-, Ruchottes-Chambertin</td><td>Clos Saint-Jacques, Lavaut Saint-Jacques, Cazetiers</td><td>Powerful, dark, structured</td></tr>
<tr><td>Morey-Saint-Denis</td><td>Clos de la Roche, Clos Saint-Denis, Clos de Tart, Clos des Lambrays, part of Bonnes-Mares</td><td>Clos de la Bussière</td><td>Between Gevrey's power and Chambolle's grace</td></tr>
<tr><td>Chambolle-Musigny</td><td>Musigny, Bonnes-Mares</td><td>Les Amoureuses, Les Charmes</td><td>Silky, perfumed</td></tr>
<tr><td>Vougeot</td><td>Clos de Vougeot</td><td>Les Petits Vougeots</td><td>Varies by owner and slope position</td></tr>
<tr><td>Flagey-Échezeaux / Vosne-Romanée</td><td>Échezeaux, Grands Échezeaux, Romanée-Conti, La Romanée, La Tâche, Richebourg, Romanée-Saint-Vivant, La Grande Rue</td><td>Cros Parantoux, Les Suchots, Aux Malconsorts, Les Beaux Monts</td><td>Spice, velvet, depth; Burgundy's summit</td></tr>
<tr><td>Nuits-Saint-Georges</td><td>None</td><td>Les Saint-Georges, Les Vaucrains, Les Pruliers</td><td>Earthy, firm</td></tr></table>
<p>Cros Parantoux is the vineyard Henri Jayer made legendary. Romanée-Conti and La Tâche are monopoles of Domaine de la Romanée-Conti (DRC).</p>

<h3>The Côte de Nuits, village by village</h3>
<p><b>Marsannay and Fixin</b>, at the edge of Dijon's suburbs, have gentler slopes and deeper soils, and no Grand Cru. Marsannay is the only village appellation in the Côte that covers red, white and rosé.</p>
<p><b>Gevrey-Chambertin</b> is the largest village of the Côte de Nuits and has nine Grands Crus, more than any other. They lie in a continuous band above the road south of the village. Chambertin and Clos de Bèze, side by side at the center, are the two that may use the name without a prefix, and Clos de Bèze, first planted by monks in the seventh century, may also be sold as Chambertin. The seven others attach Chambertin to their own names. The best Premiers Crus, Clos Saint-Jacques above all, lie on a separate south-facing slope in the Combe de Lavaux and are priced like Grands Crus; Clos Saint-Jacques missed the top rank, by tradition, because its owner in the 1930s declined to apply for it. Gevrey's wines are the darkest and most structured of the Côte.</p>
<p><b>Morey-Saint-Denis</b> is small and long overlooked because its wine was sold under its neighbors' names before the appellations. It has four Grands Crus of its own and a sliver of Bonnes-Mares. Clos de Tart, founded by Cistercian nuns in 1141, has never been divided and remains a monopole. The style sits between Gevrey's firmness and Chambolle's delicacy.</p>
<p><b>Chambolle-Musigny</b> has the most limestone in its soil and the least clay, and gives the most fragrant, fine-textured red in Burgundy. <b>Musigny</b>, on a ledge above the Clos de Vougeot, is its great vineyard and the only Grand Cru of the Côte de Nuits allowed to make a white. <b>Bonnes-Mares</b>, at the other end of the village on heavier soil, is broader and more powerful, closer to Morey. The Premier Cru Les Amoureuses, just below Musigny, is another that sells at Grand Cru prices.</p>
<p><b>Vougeot</b> is almost entirely its one walled Grand Cru. Because the <b>Clos de Vougeot</b> runs from the top of the slope to the bottom, the quality of a bottle depends on where in the Clos the grower's rows lie and on the grower.</p>
<p><b>Vosne-Romanée</b> and the neighboring commune of Flagey-Échezeaux share eight Grands Crus. Six small ones sit together in the center of the slope behind the village: Romanée-Conti (1.8 hectares) and La Tâche, both monopoles of the Domaine de la Romanée-Conti; La Romanée, the smallest appellation in France at under a hectare; La Grande Rue; Richebourg; and Romanée-Saint-Vivant. Échezeaux, large and variable, and Grands Échezeaux, smaller and more consistent, lie to the north. The wines join depth, spice and a silky texture, and command the highest prices in the wine world. Cros Parantoux, a steep, rocky Premier Cru above Richebourg, was reclaimed from scrub and replanted after the war by Henri Jayer, who made it famous.</p>
<p><b>Nuits-Saint-Georges</b> gives its name to the whole Côte and has no Grand Cru. The usual explanation is that its leading grower in the 1930s, Henri Gouges, who helped draw up the classification, thought it improper to promote his own village's vineyards. Les Saint-Georges, at the southern end, is the one most often proposed for promotion. The wines are firm, dark and earthy, and need time.</p>

<h3>Côte de Beaune: 8 Grand Crus, the great whites</h3>
<table class="t"><tr><th>Village</th><th>Grand Crus</th><th>Premiers Crus to know</th><th>Style</th></tr>
<tr><td>Ladoix, Aloxe-Corton, Pernand-Vergelesses</td><td>Corton (red, a little white), Corton-Charlemagne, Charlemagne</td><td>Île des Vergelesses</td><td>Corton: the only red GC in the Côte de Beaune</td></tr>
<tr><td>Savigny-lès-Beaune, Chorey</td><td>None</td><td>Les Lavières</td><td>Light reds, value</td></tr>
<tr><td>Beaune</td><td>None</td><td>Grèves, Clos des Mouches, Bressandes</td><td>Négociant headquarters; Hospices</td></tr>
<tr><td>Pommard</td><td>None</td><td>Les Rugiens, Les Grands Épenots</td><td>Firm, tannic reds</td></tr>
<tr><td>Volnay</td><td>None</td><td>Clos des Chênes, Caillerets, Taillepieds, Santenots (in Meursault)</td><td>Perfumed, delicate reds</td></tr>
<tr><td>Monthélie, Auxey-Duresses, Saint-Romain</td><td>None</td><td>-</td><td>Value reds and whites</td></tr>
<tr><td>Meursault</td><td>None</td><td>Perrières, Charmes, Genevrières, Gouttes d'Or</td><td>Rich, nutty whites</td></tr>
<tr><td>Puligny-Montrachet</td><td>Montrachet, Chevalier-Montrachet, Bâtard-Montrachet, Bienvenues-Bâtard-Montrachet</td><td>Les Pucelles, Les Combettes, Les Folatières, Les Caillerets</td><td>Precise, floral, mineral</td></tr>
<tr><td>Chassagne-Montrachet</td><td>Montrachet, Bâtard-Montrachet, Criots-Bâtard-Montrachet</td><td>Morgeot, Les Ruchottes, Les Caillerets</td><td>Rounder whites; also reds</td></tr>
<tr><td>Saint-Aubin, Santenay, Maranges</td><td>None</td><td>Saint-Aubin En Remilly</td><td>Value; Saint-Aubin whites</td></tr></table>
<p>Montrachet and Bâtard-Montrachet straddle Puligny and Chassagne. That's 24 + 8 = 32 in the Côte d'Or, plus Chablis Grand Cru = 33.</p>

<h3>The Côte de Beaune, village by village</h3>
<p>The <b>hill of Corton</b> is shared by three villages and carries two large Grands Crus. <b>Corton</b>, the biggest Grand Cru in Burgundy at about 100 hectares, is mainly red and is divided into named climats that may appear on the label, such as Clos du Roi, Bressandes and Renardes. <b>Corton-Charlemagne</b>, on the upper slopes, is white. The story attached to it is that Charlemagne, who owned vines there, had part of the hill planted with white grapes so that wine would not stain his white beard.</p>
<p><b>Beaune</b> is the commercial capital, home of the merchant houses and the Hospices, with a wide band of Premiers Crus on the slope behind the town and no Grand Cru. <b>Pommard</b> and <b>Volnay</b> are the best reds of the Côte de Beaune and have none either, though Rugiens in Pommard and Clos des Chênes and Caillerets in Volnay are regularly spoken of in those terms. A curiosity links Volnay to Meursault: red wine from the Santenots vineyard, which lies in Meursault, is sold as Volnay-Santenots, because Meursault's name is associated with white.</p>
<p><b>Meursault</b> is the largest white-wine village. Its three best Premiers Crus, Perrières, Genevrières and Charmes, lie at its southern end next to Puligny, and Perrières in particular is treated as a Grand Cru in all but law. <b>Puligny-Montrachet</b> and <b>Chassagne-Montrachet</b> share the Grands Crus described in the previous chapter. Puligny makes almost only white wine. Chassagne was mostly red until the late twentieth century and still makes a fair amount, from the heavier soils at the southern end of the village.</p>
<p>The villages off the main slope are where to look for value. <b>Saint-Aubin</b>, in a side valley behind Puligny and Chassagne, has high, cool, stony vineyards that have come into their own as the climate warmed. <b>Savigny</b>, <b>Monthélie</b>, <b>Auxey-Duresses</b> and <b>Saint-Romain</b> make lighter wines of both colors, and <b>Santenay</b> and <b>Maranges</b> at the southern tip make sturdy reds.</p>

<h3>Why the Grand Crus are where they are</h3>
<p>The best sites sit mid-slope (about 250-300 m), facing east to southeast. There, Jurassic limestone and marl drain well, the morning sun warms the vines early, and cold air drains away downslope. The valley floor below the D974 road is flatter, richer and wetter: generic Bourgogne territory.</p>

<p>Three things combine at mid-slope, and the classification is in effect a map of where all three are present. The first is <b>soil depth</b>. At the top there is too little soil over hard rock; at the bottom there is too much, and it is rich and wet. In the middle, thirty to eighty centimeters of stony, limy soil over fractured limestone gives good drainage with a steady reserve of water below. The second is <b>aspect</b>. The slope faces east to southeast, so the vines are warmed from sunrise, dry quickly after dew or rain, and are sheltered by the hill from the prevailing westerly weather. The third is <b>air drainage</b>. Cold air is heavy and flows downhill, so on still spring nights frost pools on the plain and at the foot of the slope, while the middle stays a degree or two warmer.</p>
<p>The road that runs the length of the Côte, the D974, marks the boundary with surprising accuracy. Above it lie the village, Premier Cru and Grand Cru vineyards; below it, on the flat, the land is regional Bourgogne. In several places the classification steps down within a few meters, from Grand Cru to Premier Cru to village, as the slope flattens and the soil deepens, and a taster can follow the change in the glass: the wines lose concentration and length in the same order.</p>
<p>The classification has faults. It was drawn by vineyard name, and some names cover uneven ground, the Clos de Vougeot, Échezeaux and Corton most obviously. A few Premiers Crus, among them Clos Saint-Jacques, Les Amoureuses, Meursault-Perrières and Cros Parantoux, are plainly of Grand Cru quality. And because it ranks land and not people, it says nothing about the difference between a careful grower and a careless one, which in practice is larger than the difference between adjacent tiers.</p>

<h3>Producers to know</h3>
<p>Reds: Domaine de la Romanée-Conti, Leroy, Armand Rousseau, Georges Roumier, Comte Liger-Belair, Dujac, Méo-Camuzet, Mugnier, de Vogüé, Ponsot, Fourrier, Lafarge (Volnay), de Montille. Whites: Coche-Dury, Roulot, Comtes Lafon, Leflaive, Ramonet, Pierre-Yves Colin-Morey, Bonneau du Martray (Corton-Charlemagne). Négociants: Louis Jadot, Joseph Drouhin, Bouchard Père & Fils, Faiveley, Louis Latour.</p>

<p>A candidate is expected to connect a few names with their villages and their signature wines. <b>Domaine de la Romanée-Conti</b> in Vosne owns the two monopoles and parts of four other Grands Crus, and is the reference for the whole region. <b>Armand Rousseau</b> is the leading name in Gevrey, with large holdings in Chambertin and Clos de Bèze. <b>Roumier</b>, <b>Mugnier</b> and <b>de Vogüé</b> are the Chambolle estates, the last the largest owner of Musigny. <b>Leroy</b> is known for biodynamic farming and very low yields. Among the whites, <b>Coche-Dury</b>, <b>Roulot</b> and <b>Lafon</b> are in Meursault, <b>Leflaive</b> in Puligny and <b>Ramonet</b> in Chassagne. The large merchant houses of Beaune own substantial vineyards of their own as well as buying fruit, and their bottlings are the most widely available route into the region.</p>

<h3>Burgundy and UNESCO</h3>
<p>The climats of Burgundy were inscribed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 2015, the same year as the Champagne hillsides, houses and cellars.</p>
<p>The listing recognized the system and not just the scenery. What UNESCO inscribed was the 1,247 climats of the Côte d'Or, each a precisely bounded and named parcel, together with the towns of Beaune and Dijon that governed the trade. The citation describes them as a cultural landscape, the product of people marking and naming differences in the ground over nearly two thousand years. No other wine region has divided its land so finely or kept the divisions so long, and the fact that the boundaries drawn by monks still decide the price of a bottle is the best evidence that they recorded something real.</p>
`,
floor:`<p>Guests ask "what's the difference between village and Premier Cru?" Give a one-line answer: site. Then compare two you have by the glass. Remember Burgundy prices: a top Premier Cru from a great grower can cost more than a négociant's Grand Cru.</p>`,
analyst:`<p>WSET Diploma expects you to explain the slope position and soils behind the hierarchy and the economics of fragmented ownership, négociants and domaines.</p>`,
terms:[["Cros Parantoux","Vosne-Romanée Premier Cru made famous by Henri Jayer."],["DRC","Domaine de la Romanée-Conti."],["Hospices de Beaune","Charity estate whose wines are auctioned each November."],["Mid-slope","The prime band of a Côte d'Or hillside where Grand Crus sit."]],
quiz:[
[1,"How many Grand Crus are in Gevrey-Chambertin?","Nine",["Two","Five","Twelve"],"All bear the Chambertin name."],
[2,"Les Amoureuses is a famous Premier Cru in:","Chambolle-Musigny",["Vosne-Romanée","Gevrey-Chambertin","Meursault"],"Often priced like a Grand Cru."],
[2,"Which Grand Cru straddles Puligny and Chassagne?","Bâtard-Montrachet",["Chevalier-Montrachet","Corton-Charlemagne","Criots-Bâtard-Montrachet"],"Montrachet also straddles both."],
[1,"Which village is known for perfumed, delicate reds?","Volnay",["Pommard","Gevrey-Chambertin","Meursault"],"Pommard is firmer."],
[2,"Where do Burgundy's Grand Crus usually sit on the slope?","Mid-slope, east/southeast facing",["Valley floor, facing west","Hilltop, at the forest edge","Upper slope, facing north"],"Best drainage and sun."],
[3,"Cros Parantoux was made famous by:","Henri Jayer",["Lalou Bize-Leroy","Armand Rousseau","Aubert de Villaine"],"Vosne-Romanée."],
[2,"Burgundy's climats became a UNESCO World Heritage Site in:","2015",["1998","2005","2021"],"Same year as Champagne."]
]});

L.push({id:"r3b", unit:"R1", title:"Champagne Houses and Growers", mins:16,
summary:"The great houses, the grower revolution, and how to read a modern Champagne back label.",
body:`
<p>Champagne is two industries at once. The big houses (maisons) own a minority of the vineyards but sell most of the bottles, buying grapes from about 16,000 growers. Since the 1990s, more growers have started making their own wine, changing what the world thinks Champagne can be.</p>
<h3>The houses and their signatures</h3>
<table class="t"><tr><th>House</th><th>Founded</th><th>Signature</th></tr>
<tr><td>Ruinart</td><td>1729</td><td>The oldest house; Chardonnay focus</td></tr>
<tr><td>Moët & Chandon</td><td>1743</td><td>The largest; Dom Pérignon is its prestige cuvée</td></tr>
<tr><td>Veuve Clicquot</td><td>1772</td><td>Barbe-Nicole Clicquot developed the riddling table (1816) and the first known blended rosé (1818); La Grande Dame</td></tr>
<tr><td>Louis Roederer</td><td>1776</td><td>Cristal, created in 1876 for Tsar Alexander II in a clear bottle; large estate vineyards</td></tr>
<tr><td>Bollinger</td><td>1829</td><td>Pinot Noir-led, barrel fermentation; R.D. (récemment dégorgé, late disgorged); Vieilles Vignes Françaises from ungrafted vines</td></tr>
<tr><td>Krug</td><td>1843</td><td>All wines barrel-fermented; Grande Cuvée blends many vintages; Clos du Mesnil, Clos d'Ambonnay</td></tr>
<tr><td>Pol Roger</td><td>1849</td><td>Cuvée Sir Winston Churchill (Churchill's favorite house)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Laurent-Perrier</td><td>1812</td><td>Saignée rosé; Grand Siècle (multi-vintage prestige)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Taittinger</td><td>1734 (as Forest-Fourneaux)</td><td>Comtes de Champagne Blanc de Blancs</td></tr>
<tr><td>Billecart-Salmon</td><td>1818</td><td>Delicate style; famous rosé</td></tr>
<tr><td>Salon</td><td>1911</td><td>One wine: vintage Blanc de Blancs from Le Mesnil, only in great years</td></tr></table>
<h3>Dom Pérignon, the man</h3>
<p>The Benedictine monk at the Abbey of Hautvillers (cellarmaster 1668-1715) did not invent sparkling wine. He worked to improve blending, pressing (white juice from black grapes) and vineyard quality, and probably tried to stop bubbles, which burst bottles. The "come quickly, I am tasting stars" quote is a later advertising invention.</p>
<h3>The grower revolution</h3>
<p>Led by <b>Anselme Selosse</b> (Avize; Burgundian methods, barrel fermentation, solera-like "Substance"), growers began bottling single-village and single-vineyard wines with low dosage. Names to know: Jacques Selosse, Egly-Ouriet (Ambonnay), Pierre Péters (Le Mesnil), Larmandier-Bernier, Agrapart, Vilmart, Jérôme Prévost (La Closerie), Cédric Bouchard (Roses de Jeanne), Chartogne-Taillet, Ulysse Collin, Bérêche.</p>
<h3>Modern back labels</h3>
<p>Quality producers now print: base vintage and percentage of reserve wine, disgorgement date, dosage in g/L, grape blend, villages. A disgorgement date matters: recently disgorged wine tastes fresher; long post-disgorgement aging adds toasty, nutty notes.</p>
<h3>Other structural changes</h3>
<ul><li>The <b>échelle des crus</b> (village price ladder) was abolished as a pricing system in 2010; Grand Cru and Premier Cru remain label terms.</li>
<li><b>Réserve perpétuelle</b>: a perpetual blend of reserve wines topped up each year (Krug and many growers use versions of this).</li>
<li>Climate change: harvests now often start in August; growers trial new frost- and heat-tolerant varieties.</li></ul>
<h3>Who owns what</h3>
<table class="t"><tr><th>Owner</th><th>Houses</th></tr>
<tr><td>LVMH</td><td>Moët &amp; Chandon, Dom Pérignon, Veuve Clicquot, Ruinart, Krug, Mercier</td></tr>
<tr><td>Pernod Ricard</td><td>Perrier-Jouët, G.H. Mumm</td></tr>
<tr><td>Laurent-Perrier group</td><td>Laurent-Perrier, Salon, Delamotte</td></tr>
<tr><td>Vranken-Pommery Monopole</td><td>Pommery, Vranken, Heidsieck &amp; Co Monopole</td></tr>
<tr><td>Lanson-BCC</td><td>Lanson, Philipponnat, Boizel, De Venoge</td></tr>
<tr><td>EPI</td><td>Charles Heidsieck, Piper-Heidsieck, Rare</td></tr>
<tr><td>Family-owned</td><td>Louis Roederer (and Deutz), Bollinger (and Ayala), Pol Roger, Taittinger, Billecart-Salmon</td></tr>
<tr><td>Cooperatives</td><td>Terroirs et Vignerons de Champagne (Nicolas Feuillatte, Castelnau, Henriot), Mailly Grand Cru, Palmer &amp; Co</td></tr></table>
<h3>The economics in five facts</h3>
<ul><li>About 34,000 hectares. Roughly 16,000 growers own about 90% of them; the houses own about 10% but sell about 70% of the bottles.</li>
<li>Shipments were <b>266 million bottles in 2025</b>, down from a peak of about 325 million in 2022. France took about 114 million and exports about 152 million.</li>
<li><b>The yield lever.</b> Each summer the Comité Champagne sets the marketable yield for the coming harvest to match sales: 12,000 kg/ha in 2022, 11,400 in 2023, 10,000 in 2024, 9,000 in 2025 and 8,800 in 2026.</li>
<li>Grapes above that limit can be held as a reserve and released in a short year.</li>
<li>It takes about 1.2 kilograms of grapes to make a bottle, and Champagne's grapes are the most expensive in the world.</li></ul>
<h3>House styles</h3>
<table class="t"><tr><th>Style</th><th>Houses</th><th>Why</th></tr>
<tr><td>Light and fresh</td><td>Laurent-Perrier, Taittinger, Ruinart, Billecart-Salmon</td><td>Chardonnay-led, stainless steel, little oxygen</td></tr>
<tr><td>Middle</td><td>Moët &amp; Chandon, Pol Roger, Louis Roederer</td><td>Balanced blends of all three grapes</td></tr>
<tr><td>Full and toasty</td><td>Bollinger, Krug, Veuve Clicquot, Charles Heidsieck</td><td>Pinot-led, with oak or a high share of old reserve wine</td></tr></table>
<h3>More prestige cuvées and firsts</h3>
<ul><li><b>Dom Pérignon</b>'s first vintage was 1921, released in 1936: the first prestige cuvée sold to the public.</li>
<li><b>Pommery</b> shipped the first commercially successful Brut, Pommery Nature, in 1874. Before it, Champagne was sweet.</li>
<li><b>Clos des Goisses</b> (Philipponnat, since 1935) was the first single-vineyard Champagne: a steep, south-facing wall at Mareuil-sur-Aÿ.</li>
<li><b>Belle Epoque</b> (Perrier-Jouët) wears the anemones Émile Gallé designed in 1902.</li>
<li><b>Numbered non-vintage.</b> Jacquesson's 700 series, Krug's Édition numbers and Roederer's Collection each identify the year's blend, so a non-vintage wine can be tracked and aged like a vintage.</li></ul>
<h3>Grower vocabulary</h3>
<ul><li><b>Special Club:</b> since 1971 the Club Trésors de Champagne lets member growers bottle their best vintage wine in a shared bottle shape, after a tasting panel approves it.</li>
<li><b>Lieu-dit</b> and <b>parcellaire:</b> wine from one named plot.</li>
<li><b>Brut Nature:</b> no dosage. Riper grapes have made it far more common.</li>
<li><b>Côte des Bar:</b> the southern district, on Kimmeridgian marl, once the houses' source of anonymous Pinot Noir and now home to sought-after growers such as Cédric Bouchard and Vouette et Sorbée.</li>
<li><b>Coteaux Champenois:</b> still wine, back in fashion as the climate warms.</li></ul>`,
floor:`<p>On the floor, match house style to guest: Bollinger or Krug for rich and toasty; Ruinart or Billecart for delicate; a grower Blanc de Blancs for the curious. For a celebration on a budget, a top Crémant or grower NV beats a big-brand NV.</p>`,
analyst:`<p>Diploma D4 asks about house styles, reserve wines, the economics of grapes versus brands, and the rise of grower Champagne. Explain how reserve wine creates consistency in NV.</p>`,
terms:[["Maison","A Champagne house, usually a négociant-manipulant."],["Récemment dégorgé","Late disgorged (R.D. is Bollinger's trademark)."],["Réserve perpétuelle","Perpetual reserve blend topped up annually."],["Disgorgement date","When the lees were removed; printed by many producers."],["Special Club","Growers' shared-bottle prestige cuvée, approved by a tasting panel."],["Marketable yield","The amount per hectare the Comité Champagne lets producers turn into Champagne each year."],["Prestige cuvée","A house's top wine, such as Dom Pérignon or Cristal."],["Clos des Goisses","Philipponnat's steep vineyard; the first single-vineyard Champagne."],["Côte des Bar","Champagne's southern district, on Kimmeridgian marl."]],
quiz:[
[1,"Which is the oldest Champagne house?","Ruinart",["Moët & Chandon","Krug","Bollinger"],"Ruinart was founded in 1729, the first house set up to make sparkling Champagne."],
[2,"Cristal was created in 1876 for:","Tsar Alexander II",["Napoleon III","Queen Victoria","Winston Churchill"],"Louis Roederer."],
[2,"Which house barrel-ferments all its wines and makes Clos du Mesnil?","Krug",["Pol Roger","Taittinger","Moët & Chandon"],"Grande Cuvée is a multi-vintage blend."],
[2,"Madame Clicquot is credited with developing:","The riddling table",["Barrel fermentation","The crown cap","Tank method"],"Her cellar developed the riddling table (pupitre) around 1816, which made clear Champagne possible."],
[1,"Who is the pioneer of the grower Champagne movement in Avize?","Anselme Selosse",["Rémi Krug","Dom Pérignon","Christopher Merret"],"Jacques Selosse estate."],
[3,"The échelle des crus pricing system was abolished in:","2010",["1985","1996","2020"],"Grand and Premier Cru remain label terms."],
[2,"Salon makes:","Only vintage Blanc de Blancs from Le Mesnil",["Only rosé from the Montagne de Reims","Only non-vintage Brut from bought grapes","Only still Coteaux Champenois"],"Only in great years."],
[2,"Krug, Ruinart and Veuve Clicquot all belong to:","LVMH",["Pernod Ricard","Lanson-BCC","Vranken-Pommery"],"So do Moët & Chandon and Dom Pérignon."],
[2,"Each summer the Comité Champagne sets the:","Marketable yield per hectare",["Retail price per bottle","Date of disgorgement","Minimum dosage for each house"],"It matches supply to sales: 8,800 kg/ha for 2026."],
[3,"The first single-vineyard Champagne, made since 1935, is:","Clos des Goisses",["Clos du Mesnil","Clos d'Ambonnay","Clos Saint-Hilaire"],"Philipponnat's steep vineyard at Mareuil-sur-Aÿ."],
[2,"A 'Special Club' bottle is:","A grower's top vintage cuvée",["A big house's entry-level blend","A cooperative's rosé","A half bottle for airlines"],"Approved by a tasting panel of the Club Trésors de Champagne."],
[2,"Champagne shipped about how many bottles in 2025?","266 million",["66 million","466 million","1 billion"],"Down from a peak of about 325 million in 2022."],
[2,"Which house is known for a full, Pinot-led, oak-fermented style?","Bollinger",["Ruinart","Laurent-Perrier","Taittinger"],"The other three are light, Chardonnay-led styles."],
[2,"Nicolas Feuillatte is the brand of a:","Cooperative",["Family-owned house","Single grower","Luxury conglomerate"],"Terroirs et Vignerons de Champagne, formed in 2022."],
[2,"Which house shipped the first commercially successful Brut Champagne, in 1874?","Pommery",["Krug","Salon","Mercier"],"Until then Champagne was sold sweet."]
]});

L.push({id:"r5b", unit:"R1", title:"The Rest of France: Southwest, Corsica, Savoie and Beyond", mins:13,
summary:"Cahors, Madiran, Jurançon, Gascony, Corsica, Savoie and France's sweet and offbeat corners.",
body:`
<h3>South West France</h3>
<p>Often called France's "museum of grapes," the Southwest kept dozens of native varieties because it sits apart from the Bordeaux machine.</p>
<ul><li><b>Bergerac and Monbazillac:</b> Bordeaux varieties next door to Bordeaux; Monbazillac is botrytized sweet white like a softer Sauternes.</li>
<li><b>Cahors:</b> Malbec (Côt, or Auxerrois locally), at least 70%. The historic "black wine" of London. Argentina's Malbec success revived it.</li>
<li><b>Madiran:</b> Tannat; fiercely tannic. Patrick Ducournau invented micro-oxygenation here in 1991 to soften it. Producer: Château Montus (Alain Brumont).</li>
<li><b>Jurançon:</b> Petit Manseng and Gros Manseng in the Pyrenees foothills; dry (Jurançon Sec) and sweet (late-harvested, passerillé on the vine), with high acidity.</li>
<li><b>Gaillac:</b> Mauzac, Len de l'El, Duras, Braucol (Fer Servadou); also sparkling.</li>
<li><b>Irouléguy:</b> Basque reds from Tannat and Cabernet Franc.</li>
<li><b>Côtes de Gascogne IGP:</b> crisp whites from Colombard, Ugni Blanc, Gros Manseng; the region also makes Armagnac.</li></ul>
<h3>Corsica</h3>
<p>Mountainous Mediterranean island, culturally Italian-French. <b>Nielluccio</b> (Sangiovese) in Patrimonio, <b>Sciaccarellu</b> in Ajaccio (peppery, pale), and <b>Vermentinu</b> (Vermentino) whites; Muscat du Cap Corse.</p>
<h3>Savoie and Bugey</h3>
<p>Alpine whites from Jacquère, Altesse (Roussette de Savoie), Bergeron (Roussanne, in Chignin-Bergeron); reds from Mondeuse. Light, fresh wines for fondue and raclette. Bugey Cerdon: pink, sweet ancestral-method sparkling.</p>
<h3>Other corners</h3>
<ul><li><b>Lorraine and Moselle:</b> pale gris wines (Côtes de Toul).</li>
<li><b>Die:</b> Clairette de Die, a sweet, Muscat-based ancestral-method sparkling, and Crémant de Die.</li>
<li><b>Cognac and Armagnac:</b> brandy country, covered in Beyond Wine.</li>
<li><b>Pineau des Charentes and Floc de Gascogne:</b> grape must fortified with Cognac or Armagnac (mistelles).</li></ul>
<h3>Why these regions stayed small</h3>
<ul><li>For centuries Bordeaux held royal privileges, the <b>police des vins</b>, that kept wine from upriver (the "High Country": Cahors, Gaillac, Bergerac) out of the port until Bordeaux's own wine had sold. The rules lasted until 1776.</li>
<li>Phylloxera, then the frost of <b>1956</b>, almost finished Cahors. It was replanted and became an AOC only in 1971.</li>
<li>Isolation had one benefit: growers kept grapes found nowhere else.</li></ul>
<h3>The grapes in the glass</h3>
<table class="t"><tr><th>Grape</th><th>Where</th><th>Markers</th></tr>
<tr><td>Malbec (Côt)</td><td>Cahors</td><td>Black plum, ink, earth, firm tannin. Leaner and more tannic than Mendoza</td></tr>
<tr><td>Tannat</td><td>Madiran, Irouléguy</td><td>Black fruit and the highest tannin in France; needs age or air</td></tr>
<tr><td>Négrette</td><td>Fronton</td><td>Violet, licorice, soft</td></tr>
<tr><td>Fer Servadou</td><td>Gaillac, Marcillac</td><td>Red currant, pepper, a rustic iron note</td></tr>
<tr><td>Petit Manseng</td><td>Jurançon</td><td>Pineapple, honey, spice, piercing acidity; sweet without botrytis</td></tr>
<tr><td>Colombard, Ugni Blanc</td><td>Gascony</td><td>Light, zesty, grapefruit; cheap and cheerful</td></tr>
<tr><td>Jacquère</td><td>Savoie</td><td>Light, alpine, barely 11%; lemon and wet stone</td></tr>
<tr><td>Altesse</td><td>Savoie</td><td>Fuller, with pear, honey and almond</td></tr>
<tr><td>Mondeuse</td><td>Savoie</td><td>Dark berries, black pepper, violet; like a lean northern Syrah</td></tr>
<tr><td>Nielluccio</td><td>Corsica</td><td>Sangiovese: sour cherry, herbs, firm acid</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sciaccarellu</td><td>Corsica</td><td>Pale, peppery, red-fruited</td></tr>
<tr><td>Vermentinu</td><td>Corsica</td><td>Citrus, herbs, a bitter-almond finish</td></tr></table>
<h3>At the table</h3>
<table class="t"><tr><th>Wine</th><th>Dish</th></tr>
<tr><td>Cahors, Madiran</td><td>Cassoulet, duck confit, magret</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sweet Jurançon</td><td>Foie gras, blue cheese</td></tr>
<tr><td>Dry Jurançon, Irouléguy</td><td>Ossau-Iraty, the Basque sheep cheese</td></tr>
<tr><td>Savoie whites</td><td>Fondue, raclette, lake fish</td></tr>
<tr><td>Corsican reds and rosés</td><td>Charcuterie, wild boar, grilled fish</td></tr>
<tr><td>Bugey Cerdon, Clairette de Die</td><td>Fruit tarts, brunch</td></tr></table>
<h3>The edges of the map</h3>
<ul><li><b>Lorraine:</b> Côtes de Toul makes <b>vin gris</b>, a rosé so pale it is nearly white, from Gamay and Pinot Noir. Moselle is a tiny AOC on the German border.</li>
<li><b>The Auvergne:</b> Saint-Pourçain (whites with the local Tressallier) and Côtes d'Auvergne (Gamay on volcanic soil) are filed under the Loire but lie in the Massif Central.</li>
<li><b>New ground:</b> since planting rules were loosened in 2016, vineyards have returned to Brittany, Normandy and the Paris region.</li></ul>
<h3>Why a sommelier should care</h3>
<p>These regions are where a list finds character at a fair price: Cahors against Bordeaux, Jurançon against Sauternes, Savoie against Chablis, Corsica against Tuscany. They are also where an exam finds its hard questions, because each has a grape that grows almost nowhere else.</p>`,
floor:`<p>These are a sommelier's value secrets: Cahors for steak, Jurançon Sec for spicy food, Savoie whites for cheese. Guests love being shown something they've never heard of that over-delivers.</p>`,
analyst:`<p>WSET Diploma expects Southwest France appellations with grapes and styles. Explain micro-oxygenation and why Tannat needed it.</p>`,
terms:[["Micro-oxygenation","Controlled introduction of tiny amounts of oxygen to soften tannin, invented in Madiran."],["Passerillage","Drying grapes on the vine to concentrate sugar (Jurançon)."],["Mistelle","Unfermented grape juice fortified with spirit (Pineau des Charentes)."],["Nielluccio","Corsican name for Sangiovese."],["Police des vins","Bordeaux's old privileges that held back wine from upriver regions."],["Vin gris","A rosé so pale it is nearly white."],["Tressallier","Local white grape of Saint-Pourçain."],["Ossau-Iraty","Basque sheep's milk cheese, a classic match for Jurançon and Irouléguy."]],
quiz:[
[1,"Cahors is based on which grape?","Malbec",["Tannat","Merlot","Négrette"],"Called Côt or Auxerrois locally."],
[2,"Micro-oxygenation was invented in which appellation?","Madiran",["Cahors","Bordeaux","Gaillac"],"1991, for Tannat."],
[2,"Jurançon's grapes are:","Petit and Gros Manseng",["Sémillon and Sauvignon Blanc","Mauzac and Len de l'El","Chenin and Chardonnay"],"Dry and sweet styles."],
[2,"Pineau des Charentes is made by:","Fortifying grape must with Cognac",["Distilling Ugni Blanc twice","Refermenting in the bottle","Leaving grapes to botrytize"],"A mistelle: unfermented grape must fortified with Cognac, then aged in oak."],
[3,"Sciaccarellu is a red grape of:","Corsica",["Savoie","Jura","Gascony"],"Peppery, pale reds from Ajaccio."],
[2,"Monbazillac is:","A botrytized sweet white near Bergerac",["A structured red from Cahors Malbec","A sparkling wine from the Drôme","A dry rosé from western Provence"],"Sémillon-based."],
[2,"Until 1776, Bordeaux's privileges hurt upriver regions such as Cahors by:","Holding their wine back from the port",["Banning Malbec anywhere outside the Médoc","Taxing every barrel made of oak","Forcing them to distil their wine"],"Upriver wine could be sold only after Bordeaux's own."],
[2,"Compared with Argentine Malbec, Cahors is usually:","Firmer, earthier and more tannic",["Sweeter and very much lower in acid","Paler and lightly sparkling","Softer, riper and more floral"],"A cooler climate and limestone give it structure."],
[2,"The classic partner for sweet Jurançon is:","Foie gras",["Oysters","Bouillabaisse","Steak tartare"],"Its acidity cuts the richness."],
[2,"The frost of 1956 nearly wiped out which appellation, which became an AOC in 1971?","Cahors",["Bandol","Sancerre","Arbois"],"It was almost entirely replanted."],
[2,"Mondeuse from Savoie tastes of:","Dark berries, pepper and violet",["Lychee, rose and ginger","Butter, toast and hazelnut","Strawberry jam and dried garrigue herbs"],"It resembles a lean northern Rhône Syrah."],
[2,"The speciality of Côtes de Toul in Lorraine is:","Vin gris, a very pale rosé",["A sweet fortified Muscat","A tannic red made from Tannat","A sparkling Chenin Blanc"],"Made from Gamay and Pinot Noir."],
[3,"Tressallier is a white grape found in:","Saint-Pourçain",["Jurançon","Patrimonio","Apremont"],"In the Auvergne, though the appellation is grouped with the Loire."]
]});

L.push({id:"r7b", unit:"R2", title:"The Italian DOCG Map", mins:18,
summary:"Italy's top-tier appellations region by region, with their grapes.",
body:`
<p>Italy has around 79 DOCGs and the list changes. You don't need every obscure one to pass Certified or Level 3, but Advanced, Diploma and the Italian Wine Scholar expect most. Learn them by region, each with its grape.</p>
<table class="t"><tr><th>Region</th><th>Key DOCGs (grape)</th></tr>
<tr><td>Piedmont</td><td>Barolo, Barbaresco, Roero, Gattinara, Ghemme (Nebbiolo); Barbera d'Asti, Nizza, Barbera del Monferrato Superiore (Barbera); Dogliani, Diano d'Alba, Ovada (Dolcetto); Gavi (Cortese); Asti and Moscato d'Asti (Moscato Bianco); Brachetto d'Acqui; Erbaluce di Caluso; Alta Langa (traditional-method sparkling); Ruchè di Castagnole Monferrato</td></tr>
<tr><td>Lombardy</td><td>Franciacorta (traditional method); Oltrepò Pavese Metodo Classico; Valtellina Superiore and Sforzato di Valtellina (Nebbiolo/Chiavennasca); Moscato di Scanzo</td></tr>
<tr><td>Veneto</td><td>Amarone and Recioto della Valpolicella (Corvina); Soave Superiore and Recioto di Soave (Garganega); Conegliano Valdobbiadene Prosecco and Asolo Prosecco (Glera); Bardolino Superiore; Piave Malanotte (Raboso); Lison (Friulano, shared with Friuli)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Friuli-Venezia Giulia</td><td>Ramandolo (Verduzzo, sweet); Colli Orientali del Friuli Picolit (sweet); Rosazzo (white blend)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Emilia-Romagna</td><td>Albana di Romagna (Italy's first white DOCG, 1987); Colli Bolognesi Pignoletto</td></tr>
<tr><td>Tuscany</td><td>Brunello di Montalcino, Chianti, Chianti Classico, Vino Nobile di Montepulciano, Carmignano, Morellino di Scansano, Montecucco Sangiovese (Sangiovese); Vernaccia di San Gimignano (white); Elba Aleatico Passito; Suvereto. Note: <b>Bolgheri is a DOC, not DOCG</b>.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Marche</td><td>Castelli di Jesi Verdicchio Riserva and Verdicchio di Matelica Riserva; Conero (Montepulciano); Offida; Vernaccia di Serrapetrona (sparkling red)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Umbria</td><td>Montefalco Sagrantino; Torgiano Rosso Riserva</td></tr>
<tr><td>Lazio</td><td>Cesanese del Piglio; Frascati Superiore; Cannellino di Frascati (sweet)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Abruzzo</td><td>Montepulciano d'Abruzzo Colline Teramane; Tullum</td></tr>
<tr><td>Campania</td><td>Taurasi and Aglianico del Taburno (Aglianico); Fiano di Avellino; Greco di Tufo</td></tr>
<tr><td>Basilicata</td><td>Aglianico del Vulture Superiore</td></tr>
<tr><td>Puglia</td><td>Primitivo di Manduria Dolce Naturale (the dry Primitivo di Manduria is DOC); Castel del Monte Nero di Troia Riserva, Rosso Riserva, Bombino Nero</td></tr>
<tr><td>Calabria</td><td>Cirò Classico (among the newest)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sicily</td><td>Cerasuolo di Vittoria (Nero d'Avola + Frappato), the island's only DOCG</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sardinia</td><td>Vermentino di Gallura</td></tr></table>
<h3>Patterns that make it stick</h3>
<ul><li>Piedmont, Veneto and Tuscany hold the most DOCGs; Piedmont leads.</li>
<li>Several DOCGs exist only for <b>Superiore</b> or <b>Riserva</b> versions of a DOC (Verdicchio Riserva, Aglianico del Vulture Superiore, Soave Superiore).</li>
<li>Sweet-wine DOCGs cluster in the northeast (Recioto, Ramandolo, Picolit).</li>
<li>Trentino-Alto Adige, Valle d'Aosta, Liguria and Molise have no DOCG.</li></ul>
<h3>Watch for name traps</h3>
<p>Montepulciano d'Abruzzo (grape) vs Vino Nobile di Montepulciano (town, Sangiovese). Vernaccia di San Gimignano (white) vs Vernaccia di Serrapetrona (sparkling red). Moscato d'Asti (frizzante, about 5%) vs Asti (spumante, higher pressure, about 7-9.5%).</p>
<h3>How the ladder was built</h3>
<table class="t"><tr><th>Year</th><th>Step</th></tr>
<tr><td>1963</td><td>The first national law creates DOC</td></tr>
<tr><td>1980</td><td>The first DOCGs: Brunello di Montalcino, Vino Nobile di Montepulciano, Barolo and Barbaresco</td></tr>
<tr><td>1984</td><td>Chianti follows</td></tr>
<tr><td>1992</td><td>The Goria law adds IGT below DOC and lets labels name a single vineyard (vigna)</td></tr>
<tr><td>2008</td><td>EU reform: DOCG and DOC both count as PDO; IGT counts as PGI</td></tr></table>
<ul><li>A wine must usually have been a DOC for years before it can be promoted.</li>
<li>Every DOCG wine is analyzed and tasted by a panel before bottling.</li>
<li>Each bottle carries a numbered neck strip, the <b>fascetta</b>: pink for red wine, green for white.</li></ul>
<h3>Label words inside a DOCG</h3>
<table class="t"><tr><th>Word</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
<tr><td>Classico</td><td>The historic heart of the zone</td></tr>
<tr><td>Superiore</td><td>Higher minimum alcohol, usually lower yields</td></tr>
<tr><td>Riserva</td><td>Longer minimum aging</td></tr>
<tr><td>Vigna</td><td>A single named vineyard</td></tr>
<tr><td>MGA, UGA</td><td>An officially named area inside the zone, as in Barolo and Chianti Classico</td></tr>
<tr><td>Passito</td><td>Made from dried grapes</td></tr></table>
<h3>From the grape's side</h3>
<table class="t"><tr><th>Grape</th><th>Its DOCGs</th></tr>
<tr><td>Nebbiolo</td><td>Barolo, Barbaresco, Roero, Gattinara, Ghemme, Valtellina Superiore, Sforzato</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sangiovese</td><td>Brunello, Chianti, Chianti Classico, Vino Nobile, Carmignano, Morellino, Montecucco</td></tr>
<tr><td>Aglianico</td><td>Taurasi, Aglianico del Taburno, Aglianico del Vulture Superiore</td></tr>
<tr><td>Barbera</td><td>Barbera d'Asti, Nizza, Barbera del Monferrato Superiore</td></tr>
<tr><td>Dolcetto</td><td>Dogliani, Diano d'Alba, Ovada</td></tr>
<tr><td>Montepulciano</td><td>Colline Teramane, Conero, Offida Rosso</td></tr>
<tr><td>Glera</td><td>Conegliano Valdobbiadene, Asolo</td></tr>
<tr><td>Garganega</td><td>Soave Superiore, Recioto di Soave</td></tr>
<tr><td>Verdicchio</td><td>Castelli di Jesi Riserva, Matelica Riserva</td></tr></table>
<h3>Famous wines that are not DOCG</h3>
<p>Fame and rank are different things. All of these are DOC: Bolgheri and Bolgheri Sassicaia, Valpolicella and Valpolicella Ripasso, Soave and Soave Classico, Prosecco (the large zone), every Lambrusco, Montepulciano d'Abruzzo, Langhe Nebbiolo, Barbera d'Alba, Trento sparkling, Vin Santo, and dry Primitivo di Manduria. Tignanello and the first Super Tuscans are IGT.</p>
<h3>More name traps</h3>
<ul><li><b>Cerasuolo d'Abruzzo</b> is a deep cherry-colored rosé from Montepulciano, and a DOC. <b>Cerasuolo di Vittoria</b> is a red DOCG from Sicily.</li>
<li><b>Roero</b> DOCG covers Nebbiolo reds and the white <b>Roero Arneis</b>.</li>
<li><b>Chianti</b> and <b>Chianti Classico</b> are two separate DOCGs with different rules.</li>
<li><b>Amarone</b> and <b>Recioto</b> are DOCG; <b>Ripasso</b>, made on their skins, is DOC.</li>
<li><b>Brachetto d'Acqui</b> is sweet, red and sparkling. <b>Gavi</b> is white, from Cortese.</li></ul>
<h3>How to learn it</h3>
<ol><li>First the twenty or so that appear on every wine list.</li>
<li>Then one region at a time, north to south, saying the grape aloud with each name.</li>
<li>Then test yourself from the grape's side, using the table above.</li></ol>`,
floor:`<p>DOCG matching items are classic Certified/Advanced fodder: "Which grape is used for Taurasi?" "Which DOCG is the only one in Sicily?" Drill them in the Matching room.</p>`,
analyst:`<p>The Italian Wine Scholar Unit 1 covers the north and Unit 2 the center and south; both test DOCGs, grapes and styles at this depth.</p>`,
terms:[["Albana di Romagna","Italy's first white DOCG (1987)."],["Alta Langa","Piedmont traditional-method sparkling DOCG."],["Cerasuolo di Vittoria","Sicily's only DOCG."],["Tullum","Abruzzo DOCG near Tollo."],["Fascetta","The numbered neck strip on a DOCG bottle: pink for red, green for white."],["Goria law","1992 law that added IGT and single-vineyard names."],["Vigna","A single named vineyard on an Italian label."],["Cerasuolo d'Abruzzo","Deep rosé DOC made from Montepulciano."]],
quiz:[
[1,"Which is a DOC, not a DOCG?","Bolgheri",["Brunello di Montalcino","Carmignano","Vernaccia di San Gimignano"],"Bolgheri Sassicaia is its own DOC."],
[2,"Italy's first white DOCG was:","Albana di Romagna",["Soave Superiore","Gavi","Vernaccia di San Gimignano"],"Albana di Romagna (now Romagna Albana) became Italy's first white DOCG in 1987."],
[2,"Which region has no DOCG?","Trentino-Alto Adige",["Emilia-Romagna","Friuli-Venezia Giulia","Basilicata"],"Also Valle d'Aosta, Liguria, Molise."],
[2,"The DOCG version of Primitivo di Manduria is:","Dolce Naturale (sweet)",["Riserva (long-aged)","Superiore (dry)","Rosato (pink)"],"The dry version is DOC."],
[2,"Ramandolo is a sweet DOCG from which grape?","Verduzzo",["Picolit","Garganega","Moscato"],"Ramandolo is made from Verduzzo Friulano in the hills north of Udine, in Friuli."],
[3,"Vernaccia di Serrapetrona is:","A sparkling red from Marche",["A white from Tuscany","A sweet white from Sardinia","A rosé from Puglia"],"Not to be confused with Vernaccia di San Gimignano."],
[1,"Vermentino di Gallura is in:","Sardinia",["Sicily","Liguria","Tuscany"],"Granite soils in the northeast."],
[2,"Besides Barolo, Barbaresco and Vino Nobile, which wine was among the first DOCGs in 1980?","Brunello di Montalcino",["Chianti Classico","Amarone della Valpolicella","Franciacorta"],"Chianti followed in 1984."],
[2,"The numbered strip on the neck of a DOCG red wine is:","Pink",["Green","Gold","Blue"],"Green is used for white wines."],
[2,"Valpolicella Ripasso is:","A DOC",["A DOCG","An IGT","A Vino da Tavola"],"Amarone and Recioto are the DOCGs."],
[2,"The 1992 Goria law added which tier to Italian wine law?","IGT",["DOCG","DOC","Vino da Tavola"],"It also allowed single-vineyard (vigna) names."],
[2,"Cerasuolo d'Abruzzo is:","A deep rosé from Montepulciano",["A red DOCG from eastern Sicily","A sweet white from dried grapes","A sparkling red from the Marche"],"Cerasuolo di Vittoria is the Sicilian red DOCG."],
[2,"Roero DOCG covers Nebbiolo reds and which white?","Arneis",["Cortese","Erbaluce","Timorasso"],"Cortese is the grape of Gavi."],
[2,"'Vigna' on an Italian label refers to:","A single named vineyard",["A wine from very old vines","A cooperative's top selection","A late-harvested sweet wine"],"Allowed since the 1992 law."]
]});

L.push({id:"r8b", unit:"R3", title:"Spain Beyond Rioja: The Full DO Map", mins:16,
summary:"Region by region from Galicia to the Canary Islands.",
body:`
<p>Spain has around 70 DOs plus Vinos de Pago. Learn them in seven groups.</p>
<table class="t"><tr><th>Area</th><th>Key DOs</th><th>What to know</th></tr>
<tr><td>Galicia (Atlantic)</td><td>Rías Baixas, Ribeiro, Ribeira Sacra, Valdeorras, Monterrei</td><td>Albariño, Treixadura, Godello, Mencía; rainy, green, granite and slate</td></tr>
<tr><td>Castilla y León</td><td>Ribera del Duero, Toro, Rueda, Bierzo, Cigales, Arribes, Tierra de León</td><td>The Duero's high plateau; Tempranillo, Verdejo, Mencía, Prieto Picudo</td></tr>
<tr><td>The Upper Ebro</td><td>Rioja (DOCa), Navarra, Basque Txakoli DOs</td><td>Rioja and the Basque coast's spritzy Hondarrabi Zuri</td></tr>
<tr><td>Aragón</td><td>Somontano, Campo de Borja, Calatayud, Cariñena</td><td>Old-vine Garnacha value; Somontano grows international grapes in the Pyrenees foothills</td></tr>
<tr><td>Catalonia</td><td>Priorat (DOQ), Montsant, Penedès, Terra Alta, Costers del Segre, Empordà, Conca de Barberà, DO Catalunya; Cava (multi-region)</td><td>Garnacha and Cariñena; white Garnacha in Terra Alta; Montsant wraps around Priorat at lower prices</td></tr>
<tr><td>The Levante and Center</td><td>Valencia, Utiel-Requena, Alicante, Jumilla, Yecla, Bullas, La Mancha, Valdepeñas, Manchuela, Vinos de Madrid</td><td>Bobal in Utiel-Requena; Monastrell; Alicante's Fondillón (aged, naturally sweet Monastrell); Sierra de Gredos old-vine Garnacha</td></tr>
<tr><td>Andalucía</td><td>Jerez-Xérès-Sherry, Manzanilla-Sanlúcar de Barrameda, Montilla-Moriles, Málaga, Sierras de Málaga, Condado de Huelva</td><td>Fortified and sweet traditions; Málaga's sweet Moscatel and PX</td></tr>
<tr><td>Islands</td><td>Binissalem, Pla i Llevant (Mallorca); Tenerife DOs, Lanzarote, La Palma</td><td>Canary Islands: ungrafted vines in volcanic soils, Listán Negro and Listán Blanco; Lanzarote vines in black-ash pits behind stone walls</td></tr></table>
<h3>Vinos de Pago</h3>
<p>Single-estate appellations with their own rules, mostly in Castilla-La Mancha and Navarra (e.g., Dominio de Valdepusa, the first, in 2003). Prestige varies; it's a legal category, not a guarantee.</p>
<h3>The new Spain</h3>
<p>The most exciting movement is a return to old vines and native grapes: Gredos Garnacha (Comando G), Ribeira Sacra Mencía, Canary Islands volcanic wines (Envínate, Suertes del Marqués), Bierzo village and single-site wines (Raúl Pérez, Descendientes de J. Palacios), and Rioja's new village and Viñedo Singular labels.</p>
<h3>The islands in more detail</h3>
<ul><li><b>The Canaries</b> never had phylloxera, so vines are ungrafted and often very old.</li>
<li><b>Lanzarote:</b> at La Geria each vine sits in its own pit dug through black volcanic ash, behind a low crescent wall that blocks the wind. The ash traps the dew. The grape is Malvasía Volcánica.</li>
<li><b>Tenerife:</b> in the Valle de la Orotava vines are trained in the <b>cordón trenzado</b>, braided cordons many meters long. Vineyards climb the slopes of Mount Teide to about 1,700 meters.</li>
<li><b>Grapes:</b> Listán Blanco is Palomino. Listán Negro gives pale, peppery, smoky reds. Negramoll is Madeira's Tinta Negra.</li>
<li><b>Mallorca:</b> native reds Manto Negro and Callet, and the white Prensal.</li></ul>
<h3>Four styles worth a closer look</h3>
<ul><li><b>Sierra de Gredos.</b> Old bush-vine Garnacha on granite at 800 to 1,200 meters, west of Madrid. Pale, perfumed and fine-boned, nearer to Pinot Noir than to Châteauneuf. It straddles three appellations: Méntrida, Vinos de Madrid and Cebreros.</li>
<li><b>Ribeira Sacra.</b> Slate terraces dropping into the gorges of the Sil and Miño, worked by hand. This is "heroic viticulture". Mencía here is light, floral and fresh.</li>
<li><b>Txakoli.</b> Three Basque DOs: Getariako, Bizkaiko and Arabako. Light, tart, slightly spritzy, about 11%.</li>
<li><b>Fondillón.</b> Alicante's historic wine: Monastrell left to overripen on the vine, fermented to at least 16% with no spirit added, then aged at least ten years in old casks, often in a solera. Nutty and off-dry, like an unfortified tawny.</li></ul>
<h3>Producers to know</h3>
<table class="t"><tr><th>Area</th><th>Names</th></tr>
<tr><td>Rías Baixas</td><td>Pazo de Señoráns, Do Ferreiro, Zárate</td></tr>
<tr><td>Valdeorras, Ribeira Sacra</td><td>Rafael Palacios (As Sortes), Guímaro, Dominio do Bibei</td></tr>
<tr><td>Bierzo</td><td>Descendientes de J. Palacios, Raúl Pérez</td></tr>
<tr><td>Ribera del Duero, Toro</td><td>Vega Sicilia, Pingus, Aalto; Numanthia, San Román</td></tr>
<tr><td>Rueda</td><td>Marqués de Riscal, Ossian, Belondrade</td></tr>
<tr><td>Priorat, Montsant</td><td>Álvaro Palacios, Clos Mogador, Terroir al Límit</td></tr>
<tr><td>Jumilla, Yecla</td><td>Casa Castillo, El Nido, Castaño</td></tr>
<tr><td>Gredos</td><td>Comando G, Daniel Landi, Bernabeleva</td></tr>
<tr><td>Canary Islands</td><td>Envínate, Suertes del Marqués, Los Bermejos</td></tr></table>
<h3>Where the value is</h3>
<ul><li><b>Old-vine Garnacha</b> from Campo de Borja and Calatayud.</li>
<li><b>Monastrell</b> from Jumilla and Yecla.</li>
<li><b>Verdejo</b> from Rueda and <b>Godello</b> from Valdeorras as alternatives to Sauvignon Blanc and Chardonnay.</li>
<li><b>Mencía</b> from Bierzo for guests who like Pinot Noir or Cabernet Franc.</li>
<li><b>Cava</b> with long lees aging, at half the price of Champagne.</li></ul>`,
floor:`<p>Spain is the best value section of most lists. Bobal, Monastrell, Mencía, Godello and Canary reds give sommeliers interesting bottles at accessible prices.</p>`,
analyst:`<p>WSET Level 3 names Rías Baixas, Bierzo, Rueda, Toro, Ribera, Rioja, Navarra, Calatayud, Campo de Borja, Cariñena, Priorat, Penedès, Jumilla and La Mancha. The Spanish Wine Scholar goes deeper still.</p>`,
terms:[["Vino de Pago","Spanish single-estate appellation."],["Bobal","Red grape of Utiel-Requena."],["Fondillón","Aged, naturally sweet Monastrell from Alicante."],["Listán Negro","Red grape of the Canary Islands."],["Cordón trenzado","Tenerife's braided-cordon vine training."],["Malvasía Volcánica","White grape of Lanzarote."],["Gredos","Mountain range west of Madrid known for old-vine Garnacha."],["Heroic viticulture","Farming on slopes too steep for machines."]],
quiz:[
[1,"Which grape defines Utiel-Requena?","Bobal",["Mencía","Monastrell","Godello"],"Valencia region."],
[2,"Lanzarote's vines grow in:","Pits dug into volcanic ash, behind stone walls",["Terraces cut into slate above a river","Chalky albariza near the Atlantic coast","Flat red clay on a high inland plateau"],"Protects vines from wind."],
[2,"Montsant surrounds which DOQ?","Priorat",["Rioja","Ribera del Duero","Penedès"],"Similar grapes at lower prices."],
[2,"Godello is the signature white of:","Valdeorras",["Rueda","Rías Baixas","Penedès"],"Valdeorras, in eastern Galicia, revived Godello from near extinction in the 1970s."],
[3,"Spain's first Vino de Pago was:","Dominio de Valdepusa",["Vega Sicilia Único","Dominio de Pingus","Clos Mogador"],"2003, Castilla-La Mancha."],
[2,"Which DO is spread across several regions rather than tied to one area?","Cava",["Priorat","Toro","Bierzo"],"Most is from Penedès."],
[2,"Fondillón from Alicante is:","An unfortified, long-aged Monastrell",["A fortified sweet white from Moscatel","A sparkling rosé made from Bobal","A dry white made from Merseguera"],"At least 16% alcohol with no spirit added, and ten years in cask."],
[2,"In Tenerife's Valle de la Orotava, vines are trained as:","Long braided cordons",["Bush vines in pits of ash","Pergolas on granite posts","Single stakes on steep slate"],"The cordón trenzado."],
[2,"Sierra de Gredos is known for:","Pale, perfumed old-vine Garnacha",["Dense, heavily oaked Tempranillo","Salty, spritzy Hondarrabi Zuri","Sweet fortified Pedro Ximénez"],"Grown on granite at 800 to 1,200 meters."],
[2,"Listán Blanco of the Canary Islands is the same grape as:","Palomino",["Albariño","Verdejo","Malvasía"],"Sherry's grape, ungrafted on volcanic soil."],
[2,"Getariako, Bizkaiko and Arabako are the three DOs of:","Txakoli",["Cava","Sherry","Ribeiro"],"The Basque Country's light, tart white."],
[2,"Manto Negro and Callet are native red grapes of:","Mallorca",["Galicia","Tenerife","Aragón"],"Binissalem and Pla i Llevant are the island's DOs."],
[2,"Ribeira Sacra's vineyards are best described as:","Steep terraces above river gorges",["Flat plains of deep red clay","Ash pits behind curved stone walls","Sandy dunes beside the ocean"],"Along the Sil and Miño rivers."]
]});
